# The AndeSim Linux runtime

`andesim run --runtime linux <program>` runs a Linux program instead of a
bare-metal ELF. There is no Linux kernel. A small proxy kernel, vlinux,
boots first, loads your program, builds the Linux initial stack (argv and
auxv), and answers the system calls of the program.

This page covers a Linux workload from end to end, including llama.cpp.
It assumes that you already built the image, as the top-level
[README.md](../../README.md) describes.

## What you must supply

**An Andes RISC-V Linux toolchain (glibc, rv64).** The directory must
contain `bin/riscv64-linux-gcc`. This is a different toolchain from the
bare-metal one, which builds the AndeSim runtime library.

**Your program, linked statically.** vlinux has no dynamic loader.

**Every data file that the program reads.** The image holds no
application and no model.

## Rule: keep everything below the directory that you run from

A run happens inside a container. `./andesim` mounts the current
directory at the same path in the image, and mounts nothing else. Put the
program, its data files and its output below the directory that you run
from. A path outside that directory does not exist for the program.

## A first Linux program

Put this in `hello.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
        printf("hello from vlinux, argc=%d\n", argc);
        for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++)
                printf("  argv[%d] = %s\n", i, argv[i]);
        fflush(stdout);   /* flush before exit: see "Limits" below */
        return 0;
}
```

Compile it and run it:

```sh
export LINUX_TOOLCHAIN=/path/to/riscv-linux-toolchain
$LINUX_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-linux-gcc -march=rv64gc -mabi=lp64d -O2 \
    -static hello.c -o hello.elf

./andesim run --mode fast --runtime linux hello.elf -- one two
```

Everything after `--` becomes the argv of your program:

```
vlinux: ENOSYS 258
hello from vlinux, argc=3
  argv[1] = one
  argv[2] = two
```

The `ENOSYS` line is normal. See "Limits" below.

## llama.cpp

### 1. Get the source

```sh
git clone https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
```

### 2. Build it for the target

```sh
cmake -S llama.cpp -B build-rv64-static \
    -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Linux -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=riscv64 \
    -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=$LINUX_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-linux-gcc \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=$LINUX_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-linux-g++ \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS=-static \
    -DGGML_NATIVE=OFF -DGGML_OPENMP=OFF -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF \
    -DGGML_RV_ZFH=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZVFH=OFF \
    -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=-mext-zvlsseg -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-mext-zvlsseg

cmake --build build-rv64-static --target llama-completion -j"$(nproc)"
```

Each flag earns its place:

- `-static` and `BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF`: vlinux has no dynamic loader.
- `-mext-zvlsseg`: the RISC-V segment load and store intrinsics that
  ggml uses. Without this flag the Andes toolchain does not declare
  them, and the build stops in `ggml-cpu/arch/riscv/repack.cpp`.
- `GGML_NATIVE=OFF`: the host is not the target.
- `GGML_OPENMP=OFF`: vlinux runs one hart and one thread.
- `GGML_RV_ZFH=OFF` and `GGML_RV_ZVFH=OFF`: half-precision floating
  point. Turn these on only for an engine that has them.

### 3. Supply a model

AndeSim ships no model. Download a GGUF file, and put it below the
directory that you run from:

```sh
mkdir -p models
# copy or download your .gguf file into models/
```

Bonsai-4B-Q1_0 (572 MB) is a good first model. It is small enough to
load in a few minutes.

### 4. Run it

```sh
./andesim run --mode fast --runtime linux --mem-size 2G \
    build-rv64-static/bin/llama-completion \
    -- -m models/Bonsai-4B-Q1_0.gguf -no-cnv \
       --prompt "The capital of France is" -n 8 -c 512 -t 1
```

The program generates real text. Be patient: QEMU emulates the vector
instructions, so a 4B-parameter model needs minutes for the load and
minutes more for each few tokens.

## Memory

`--runtime linux` gives 1G of guest RAM when you name no size. A model
needs more: the mapped model file plus the KV cache. Bonsai-4B at
`-c 512` needs `--mem-size 2G`. If the program stops with an allocation
error, raise the size.

## Measure a region

To get a cycle count for one part of a Linux program, mark the region in
the source, exactly as the top-level README does for a bare-metal
program:

```c
#include <andesim/ROI.h>
...
andesim_ROI_begin();
        /* the part to measure */
andesim_ROI_end();
```

Add the header directory to the compiler flags:

```
-I /path/to/andesim/build/runtime/include
```

Then measure it:

```sh
./andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --runtime linux \
    --mem-size 2G --step-timeout-ms 900000 \
    build-rv64-static/bin/llama-completion \
    -- -m models/Bonsai-4B-Q1_0.gguf -no-cnv \
       --prompt "The capital of France is" -n 8 -c 512 -t 1
```

The answer is the last line, `[andesim] <N> cycles`.

Raise `--step-timeout-ms`. The default of 90000 is too short: the
cycle-accurate leg of a model kernel takes many minutes, and AndeSim
stops a leg that is silent for longer than this timeout.

AndeSim measures the FIRST time that the marked region runs, and then it
stops the run. Put the markers around one node of one layer. Markers in a
function that every layer calls give you the cycles of one call, and the
program never reaches the end of the model.

## Limits

- **Static binaries only.** vlinux has no dynamic loader.
- **One hart and one thread.** `clone` is not available. Give
  `-t 1` to llama.cpp.
- **The environment is empty.** `getenv()` finds nothing, so an option
  that comes from an environment variable never reaches the program. Use
  a command-line argument, or a compile-time value.
- **`vlinux: ENOSYS <number>` is not an error.** vlinux answers the
  system calls that a static glibc needs. Any other number returns
  `-ENOSYS` and prints one line, exactly as a real kernel does for a
  system call that it does not have. Number 258 is `riscv_hwprobe`, and
  glibc continues with generic code.
- **Flush your output before the program ends.** On the Andes glibc
  toolchain, `exit()` faults while it walks its own handler list. The
  fault comes after all the work of the program, but before glibc
  flushes `stdout`, so buffered output is lost. Call `fflush(stdout)`,
  or use `_exit()`. AndeSim prints the fault as
  `mcause: 0x2`. This is a known gap of the toolchain, not of vlinux:
  every system call up to that point is correct.
